SQL column has a time portion, so `Equals(adjustedDate)` won’t match. Filter by a **day range**:

```csharp
// adjustedDate is a DateOnly
var dayStart = adjustedDate.ToDateTime(TimeOnly.MinValue);   // yyyy-MM-dd 00:00:00
var nextDay  = dayStart.AddDays(1);                          // next midnight (open interval)

var entities = await _dispatchRepository.FindCollectionAsync(x =>
    x.RouteDate >= dayStart && x.RouteDate < nextDay);
```

That translates cleanly to SQL and ignores the time of day.

### Alternatives (SQL Server specific / EF Core)
- If you’re on SQL Server and EF Core supports it, you can use `DateTime.Date` (EF translates to `CAST(... AS date)`):

```csharp
var day = adjustedDate.ToDateTime(TimeOnly.MinValue).Date;
var entities = await _dispatchRepository.FindCollectionAsync(x => x.RouteDate.Date == day);
```

- Or `EF.Functions.DateDiffDay`:

```csharp
var day = adjustedDate.ToDateTime(TimeOnly.MinValue);
var entities = await _dispatchRepository.FindCollectionAsync(x =>
    EF.Functions.DateDiffDay(day, x.RouteDate) == 0);
```

But the **range filter** (`>= dayStart && < nextDay`) is the most portable and reliable across providers.

