If you truly use `DateOnly` on the model, there’s nothing to “strip”—`DateOnly` has no time. The place where time sneaks in is usually the **DB column (datetime)** or **DTOs**. Here are two solid fixes; pick the one that matches your setup.

---

## A) Keep `DateOnly` on the entity (recommended)  
Add an EF Core value converter so the DB always stores midnight and you always read/write dates without time.

```csharp
// in OnModelCreating
builder.Entity<Dispatch>()
    .Property(e => e.RouteDate)
    .HasConversion(
        v => v.ToDateTime(TimeOnly.MinValue),  // DateOnly -> DateTime (00:00:00)
        v => DateOnly.FromDateTime(v));        // DateTime -> DateOnly
```

That’s all you need. Your property stays:

```csharp
public DateOnly RouteDate { get; set; }
```

EF will persist/read as a `datetime` with `00:00:00`, effectively stripping any time.

---

## B) If your property must be `DateTime` (normalize in setter)
If you can’t use `DateOnly` on the entity and must keep a `DateTime`, normalize in the setter to drop the time:

```csharp
private DateTime _routeDate;
public DateTime RouteDate
{
    get => _routeDate;
    set => _routeDate = DateTime.SpecifyKind(value.Date, DateTimeKind.Unspecified);
    // or simply: _routeDate = value.Date;
}
```

This guarantees the stored value is always midnight (time stripped), no matter what comes in.

---

### Bonus: API binding to `DateOnly`
If your controller takes `DateOnly` and clients sometimes send a full `datetime`, add a binder/converter or accept a `DateTime` and convert:

```csharp
public IActionResult Get([FromQuery] DateTime date)
{
    var day = DateOnly.FromDateTime(date);
    ...
}
```

Either approach above ensures you **never carry a time** beyond the boundary where it enters your system.

